WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.629
Behind me is the last surviving tollgate in
London,

00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:05.580
on a private road through Dulwich College.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.230
Back at the start of the 19th century,

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:11.949
almost all the roads out of London were turnpikes,
toll roads.

00:00:11.949 --> 00:00:17.610
And it wasn't just London: across the country,
there were hundreds of turnpike trusts,

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:22.650
private companies that had a government-approved
monopoly on one particular bit of road.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.100
They kept it in reasonable condition,

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.510
at least by the horse-drawn standards of the day,

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:29.390
and in exchange they could charge a toll.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:31.990
But travelling across country meant you'd
be paying tolls to

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:35.170
half a dozen different and possibly-corrupt
companies as you went.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:39.289
Heck, just travelling across London you could
hit tollgate after tollgate after tollgate.

00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:44.979
Now, the Turnpike Trusts did some good: the
money paid for a decent road network.

00:00:44.979 --> 00:00:48.120
But they weren't popular: before the turnpikes
came along, the roads,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.089
although they were frequently rough and difficult
to travel on, were free.

00:00:52.089 --> 00:00:57.039
So there were complaints, and occasional riots,
but the turnpikes stood.

00:00:57.039 --> 00:00:59.149
But then along came the railways.

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:01.829
Suddenly anyone who could afford to pay road tolls

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:04.129
could also afford a faster and more comfortable option.

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:06.940
So both people and freight moved to rail.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.820
The turnpike trusts started to collapse,

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.420
local government took over all the roads,

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.360
and by the time motorways came along,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.250
it was accepted that government collected tax,

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.460
and then used that tax to build new roads.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:20.940
But what if history had turned out slightly
differently?

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:24.680
Where would we be if all the roads were still
private,

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:27.460
if every major route had a toll that went
not to government,

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:30.610
like the Congestion Charge or modern American
turnpikes,

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.810
but to a private company running it?

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:35.630
If every time you drove onto London's Westway,

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:39.850
you paid £5 to the Westway Corporation sponsored
by McDonalds?

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:43.000
Well, actually, er, we would be in France.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.310
Most of the French autoroutes, the motorways there,

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:49.850
are run by private companies, collecting tolls,
just like the old days.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:54.060
You don't need to visit some bizarre zeppelin-filled
alternate reality to find turnpikes --

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.090
there are always zeppelins in alternate realities --

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:58.040
all you need to do is cross the Channel.

